NNAAMMEE
     ffrreeeebbssdd--vveerrssiioonn — print the version and patch level of the installed sys‐
     tem

SSYYNNOOPPSSIISS
     ffrreeeebbssdd--vveerrssiioonn [--kkrruu] [--jj _j_a_i_l]

DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
     The ffrreeeebbssdd--vveerrssiioonn utility makes a best effort to determine the version
     and patch level of the installed kernel and / or userland.

     The following options are available:

     --kk          Print the version and patch level of the installed kernel.
                 Unlike uname(1), if a new kernel has been installed but the
                 system has not yet rebooted, ffrreeeebbssdd--vveerrssiioonn will print the
                 version and patch level of the new kernel.

     --rr          Print the version and patch level of the running kernel.  Un‐
                 like uname(1), this is unaffected by environment variables.

     --uu          Print the version and patch level of the installed userland.
                 These are hardcoded into ffrreeeebbssdd--vveerrssiioonn during the build.

     --jj _j_a_i_l     Print the version and patch level of the installed userland
                 in the given jail specified by _j_i_d or _n_a_m_e.  This option can
                 be specified multiple times.

     If several of the above options are specified, ffrreeeebbssdd--vveerrssiioonn will print
     the installed kernel version first, then the running kernel version, next
     the userland version, and finally the userland version of the specified
     jails, on separate lines.  If neither is specified, it will print the
     userland version only.

IIMMPPLLEEMMEENNTTAATTIIOONN NNOOTTEESS
     The ffrreeeebbssdd--vveerrssiioonn utility should provide the correct answer in the vast
     majority of cases, including on systems kept up-to-date using
     freebsd-update(8), which does not update the kernel version unless the
     kernel itself was affected by the latest patch.

     To determine the name (and hence the location) of a custom kernel, the
     ffrreeeebbssdd--vveerrssiioonn utility will attempt to parse _/_b_o_o_t_/_d_e_f_a_u_l_t_s_/_l_o_a_d_e_r_._c_o_n_f
     and _/_b_o_o_t_/_l_o_a_d_e_r_._c_o_n_f, looking for definitions of the _k_e_r_n_e_l and _b_o_o_t_f_i_l_e
     variables, both with a default value of “kernel”.  It may however fail to
     locate the correct kernel if either or both of these variables are de‐
     fined in a non-standard location, such as in _/_b_o_o_t_/_l_o_a_d_e_r_._r_c.

EENNVVIIRROONNMMEENNTT
     ROOT  Path to the root of the filesystem in which to look for _l_o_a_d_e_r_._c_o_n_f
           and the kernel.

EEXXAAMMPPLLEESS
     To determine the version of the currently running userland:

           /bin/freebsd-version -u

     To inspect a system being repaired using a live system:

           mount -rt ufs /dev/ada0p2 /mnt
           env ROOT=/mnt /mnt/bin/freebsd-version -ku

